PLACES=[
N('olana-greenport','Olana (Frederic Church\'s house and landscape)','Greenport','hud','inst','standing',42.21717,-73.82927,'exact','open','walk','','1870–1890',
  'The painter Frederic Edwin Church, a leading figure of the Hudson River School, built this house on a hilltop south of Hudson with the architect Calvert Vaux between 1870 and 1872, after a long trip through Europe and the Middle East, and added his last studio in 1888–1890. Around it he laid out a 250-acre park as if it were a painting: a lake dug out of a swamp, thousands of planted trees and about five miles of carriage drives surfaced with red shale quarried on the place. It is one of the few American artists\' homes, studios and estates to survive whole, and a National Historic Landmark since 1965. The State Office of Parks owns and runs it, with The Olana Partnership.',
  'A villa of stone and coloured brick with tile, slate and stencilled cornices in Persian and Moorish patterns that Church drew himself, looking west over the river to the Catskills. Inside, according to Wikipedia, the rooms stay much as Church left them, with his furnishings and about 40 paintings by him and his friends; the house is seen on a guided tour. The carriage drives, lake and viewpoints can be walked free.',
  'Frederic Church designed the house, the stencils and the view.',
  'New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation, with The Olana Partnership. Entrance on NY 9G about a mile south of the Rip Van Winkle Bridge. Parking at the Frederic Church Center, at the Wagon House Education Center and near the Olana shop at the top of the hill (Olana Partnership page). The landscape is free; the house is by guided tour, with tickets, and the Partnership says tour hours vary by season. A lift reaches the main floor (Parks page).',
  [W('Olana State Historic Site'),OLANAP,OLANAH],
  was='lm-olana',brick='some',hours='Grounds free, daily 8 a.m. to sunset; house by guided tour, hours vary by season'),
N('hudson-hall-opera-house','Hudson Hall (Hudson Opera House, the old city hall)','Hudson','hud','civic','standing',42.25223,-73.79042,'exact','open','drive','','1855',
  'Built in 1855 at 327 Warren Street to designs by the local architect Peter H. Avery, this was Hudson\'s first city hall, with the post office, the Franklin Library and the First National Bank of Hudson under the same roof and a public hall upstairs used for lectures, shows, dances and poultry shows. Around 1880 it took the name Opera House, as town halls across the country then did. After city hall moved up Warren Street in 1962 it became a Moose lodge and then stood empty for decades. Restored by a local non-profit, it is now an arts centre, and Wikipedia calls it New York\'s oldest surviving theatre.',
  'A brick Italianate hall in the middle of Warren Street. The upstairs hall is used for performances and the ground floor for exhibitions and events; when the building is open to walk in was not checked.',
  'The city hall that became the state\'s oldest surviving theatre.',
  'Run by the non-profit Hudson Hall. On Warren Street between Third and Fourth; street parking. Its own visit page refused to open in this session, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Hudson Hall (arts organization)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('hudson-historic-district-warren-street','Hudson Historic District: Warren Street and Promenade Hill','Hudson','hud','town','standing',42.25111,-73.78861,'area','public','walk','','1785–1930s',
  'Hudson was laid out on a grid in 1785 by New England whaling families who had bought the land from the Van Hoesens, and the downtown still follows that plan. The district, listed in 1985, covers 139 acres and 756 contributing buildings from the city\'s founding to the 1930s; Wikipedia calls Warren Street the state\'s most intact 19th-century main commercial street. The founders kept the high ground at the west end, Promenade Hill, as a "Parade" for the townspeople. Part of the 1970 Front Street district was torn down soon after it was listed and left out of the later one.',
  'Walk Warren Street from Promenade Park at the river end, past the two-storey brick houses of the founding years on Front, Union and Warren, to Courthouse Square at Fourth Street, where the 1900 county courthouse by Warren & Wetmore and the 1911 post office face each other. Whales on the street signs recall the whaling founders. Shops and restaurants fill the storefronts.',
  'A whaling captains\' grid that kept its main street whole.',
  'City streets, public at all times. Street parking (metered in places) and municipal lots. The pin is in the middle of the district by Courthouse Square.',
  [W('Hudson Historic District (New York)'),W('Hudson, New York')],
  brick='yes'),
N('hudson-post-office-ny','United States Post Office, Hudson','Hudson','hud','civic','standing',42.25051,-73.78924,'exact','open','drive','','1909–1911',
  'Built in 1909–1911 across Union Street from the Columbia County courthouse, with a portico that echoes the courthouse\'s, and enlarged in the same style in the 1930s. During that work the Treasury Relief Art Project added Vincent Glinsky\'s sculpture "Evolution of Transportation" to the lobby. It was listed on the National Register in 1988, and still serves ZIP code 12534.',
  'A one-storey building of brick in Flemish bond on a granite-faced base, with marble-trimmed arched windows and a central pavilion on South Fourth Street. Inside the L-shaped lobby is the Glinsky sculpture.',
  'A 1911 post office with a New Deal sculpture in the lobby.',
  'Working post office of the United States Postal Service, at Union and South Fourth streets. Lobby hours are not checked here; street parking.',
  [W('United States Post Office (Hudson, New York)')],
  brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('hudson-almshouse','Hudson Almshouse','Hudson','hud','inst','standing',42.25258,-73.78682,'exact','view','drive','','about 1818',
  'Built about 1818 as the city almshouse, with a large rear wing added in the 1880s. It went on to be a lunatic asylum, a female academy, a private house and the Hudson Orphan and Relief Association, and from 1959 to 2016 the Hudson Area Association Library. It was listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'A three-storey central block with two-storey wings, all of dressed limestone, in the Federal style, at 400 State Street. Its use and owner since the library left in 2016 were not checked, so treat it as closed: look from the street.',
  '',
  'Owner and present use not checked. See it from State Street; street parking.',
  [W('Hudson Almshouse')],
  brick='no'),
N('south-bay-mill-hudson','South Bay Mill','Hudson','hud','industry','standing',42.2532,-73.7959,'site','view','drive','','1860',
  'Built in 1860 as a soap and candle factory near Hudson\'s river harbour, the mill went through a series of industrial uses and ended as a large electronics factory. It was listed on the National Register in 2022.',
  'An industrial building near the South Bay end of the waterfront. Wikipedia says little about it; what it looks like and its present use were not checked, and the pin is the Register list point, not looked up on the ground.',
  '',
  'Private. See it from the street; no access to the building.',
  [W('South Bay Mill')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('hudson-station-ny','Hudson station (New York Central, 1874)','Hudson','hud','rail','standing',42.25406,-73.79776,'exact','public','drive','stations','1874',
  'The New York Central built this station in 1874, and Wikipedia calls it the oldest station in the state still in continuous use. It was also the end of the Boston and Albany\'s Hudson Branch, whose passenger trains stopped in 1932. Amtrak took over in 1971, and the state paid for a renovation in 1991–1992. Trains to New York, Albany, Montreal, Toronto and Burlington call here.',
  'A working station on the river side of town at the foot of Warren Street. What original fabric survives inside was not checked.',
  'The oldest station in New York still taking passengers.',
  'Amtrak station at 69 South Front Street; parking lots beside it and across the street. Waiting-room hours were not checked. Stay off the tracks: this is an active main line.',
  [W('Hudson station (New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('oliver-bronson-house','Dr. Oliver Bronson House','Hudson','hud','landmark','standing',42.24318,-73.78537,'exact','view','drive','','1811–1849',
  'Built in 1811–1812 as a Federal house for the Hudson merchant Samuel Plumb, it was bought in 1838 by Dr. Oliver Bronson, who had Alexander Jackson Davis remake it in 1839 and 1849 with a deep veranda, bracketed eaves and new bedrooms and parlours; it counts as the first house in the Hudson River Bracketed style. From 1904 the state used the estate as the Training School for Girls, a reformatory, and since 1976 as a men\'s prison, the Hudson Correctional Facility. The house stood derelict for twenty years until Historic Hudson took it on in 1997; it became a National Historic Landmark in 2003 and is being restored.',
  'The house and about 50 acres sit well back from the road behind a gated drive with a small gatehouse (Wikipedia). Untapped New York (2024) says much of the surrounding state land is closed, no building is open to the public yet, and Historic Hudson hopes to make the grounds a public park. Whether the house can be seen from a public road was not checked.',
  'A Davis villa on prison land, waiting for a park.',
  'State land leased to Historic Hudson, next to the Hudson Correctional Facility. Not open: do not go past the gate. The preservation group has held occasional tours and events; watch its notices.',
  [W('Oliver Bronson House'),UNTAP],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('van-hoesen-house-claverack','Jan Van Hoesen House (Van Hoesen–Marriott House)','Claverack','hud','landmark','standing',42.25642,-73.75221,'exact','view','drive','','1715–1724',
  'Jan Van Hoesen built this brick house between 1715 and 1724; his family had bought from the Mohicans in 1662 the land on which Hudson and Greenport now stand. It is one of about seven brick Dutch farmhouses of its kind left in the upper Hudson Valley. In the 1800s it was the home of the Quaker abolitionist Charles Marriott, and the African American Archive of Columbia County says it was a stop on the Underground Railroad. Long empty, it was on the Preservation League\'s "Seven to Save" list in 2009, and the State Museum dug the site in 2011.',
  'A tall, steep-roofed brick house with parapet gables and gauged arches over the openings in red and dark-glazed brick, standing above the Dutch Acres mobile home park on NY 66, just east of Claverack Creek. The garret and granary windows and a corner door to the cellar kitchen are original (Wikipedia). Hill Country Observer (June 2025) says a new non-profit, the Van Hoesen–Marriott House Preservation Project, hopes to buy and stabilise it and to offer scheduled tours, not to restore it as a museum.',
  'Hudson\'s land was bought by the man who built this house.',
  'Private: inside a mobile home park whose owner had offered to sell the house to the preservation group (Hill Country Observer, June 2025). See it from NY 66; do not walk into the park. No tours yet.',
  [W('Jan Van Hoesen House'),HCO],
  brick='yes'),
N('van-rensselaer-lower-manor-claverack','Van Rensselaer Lower Manor House','Claverack','hud','landmark','standing',42.22472,-73.72556,'site','view','drive','','18th century',
  'Two 18th-century houses, one stone and one frame, were joined with a hyphen and sided in wood to make this one; one of them was among the earliest houses in the area. The Van Rensselaers used it to hold the southern edge of their lands in colonial times, and after the Revolution it passed to the Mesick family, a descendant of which still owned it when it was listed in 1998.',
  'A long wooden-sided house on a 1.3-acre lot north of NY 23 on the east side of Claverack, with a barn listed with it. Wikipedia says it keeps much of its original layout, finishes and windows inside, and that the owners run a greenhouse and flower shop near the road; whether that is still so was not checked.',
  '',
  'Private home. See it from the road.',
  [W('Van Rensselaer Lower Manor House')],
  brick='no'),
N('cornelius-muller-house-claverack','Cornelius S. Muller House','Claverack','hud','landmark','standing',42.22222,-73.73889,'exact','view','drive','','1767',
  'A pre-Revolutionary brick house in the Dutch style with some English touches. During the Revolution the local Committee of Safety met here and courts martial were held in it. It was renovated in 1840 but has otherwise stayed as built, and was listed on the National Register in 1997.',
  'A brick house set very close to NY 23B, across the road from the old Trinity Episcopal Church, among other 18th- and 19th-century houses. Private: no interior visits.',
  'Courts martial in a Dutch brick farmhouse.',
  'Private home. See it from NY 23B; no parking lot.',
  [W('Cornelius S. Muller House')],
  brick='yes'),
N('germantown-parsonage','German Reformed Sanctity Church Parsonage (Historic Parsonage)','Germantown','hud','landmark','standing',42.14179,-73.88263,'exact','open','drive','','1740s',
  'The oldest building in the town of Germantown, built in the 1740s for the Reformed church of the Palatine Germans of East Camp, refugees whom the crown had settled here to make naval stores, and enlarged about twenty years later. Its last private owners left it to the town in 1990. It houses the town\'s history department, and Bard College has dug the grounds.',
  'A house of wood, brick and stone on Maple Avenue, about 300 m east of NY 9G. Finds from the digs are shown inside. Wikipedia says it is open to visitors on Saturdays; the town\'s own page shows only that its History Advisory Committee meets there in public on the last Saturday of each month at 10:30 a.m.',
  'East Camp\'s Palatine parsonage, the oldest building in town.',
  'Town of Germantown, 52 Maple Avenue. Visiting hours not confirmed on the town\'s page; the history committee\'s monthly meeting is open to the public.',
  [W('German Reformed Sanctity Church Parsonage'),GTOWN],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',hours='?'),
N('clarkson-chapel-clermont','Clarkson Chapel','Clermont','hud','worship','standing',42.09361,-73.89611,'exact','view','drive','','1860',
  'Levinus Clarkson, a landowner who had married into the Livingston family, built this Carpenter Gothic chapel in 1860 on his estate, Knollwood, for a group of dissenting Episcopalians, himself among them. It passed from his descendants to Columbia County. It is on the National Register and part of the Hudson River Historic District.',
  'A small board-and-batten chapel with a steep gable roof, hooded windows on scrolled brackets and pinnacles at the gable ends, with its bellcote standing apart. The Register form, as Wikipedia gives it, describes painted wooden pews, hanging lanterns, a cast-iron stove and a pump organ inside; whether it ever opens was not checked. It sits behind a row of trees on the west side of NY 9G.',
  '',
  'Columbia County (Wikipedia). About 0.7 mile south of the Stone Jug at Jug Road, on the west side of NY 9G; a driveway comes in from the south-east. Opening not checked: look from outside.',
  [W('Clarkson Chapel')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-johns-lutheran-ancram','St. John\'s Lutheran Church and horse sheds','Ancram','hud','worship','standing',42.04953,-73.63863,'site','view','drive','','1847',
  'A Greek Revival meeting-house church of heavy timber frame built in 1847, altered in 1854, 1886 and 1906; the bell tower dates from 1886. The complex keeps its parsonage of about 1853, 19th-century horse sheds and a church hall of about 1910, and was listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'A plain white frame church in Ancram hamlet with its row of horse sheds, a survival from when the congregation drove in by wagon. Service times and the interior were not checked.',
  'A country church that kept its horse sheds.',
  'Church property in the hamlet, near Simons General Store. Look from the road unless a service is on.',
  [W('St. John\'s Lutheran Church (Ancram, New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('copake-memorial-clock','Copake Memorial Clock','Copake','hud','curio','standing',42.10361,-73.54944,'exact','public','drive','','1944',
  'A four-faced pedestal chime clock put up in the hamlet in 1944 to remember those from Copake who served, after a local soldier, Steven McIntyre, was killed in Italy. His uncle Ed McIntyre and Albert C. Bristol raised the $2,600 it cost, a large sum in wartime, and it was dedicated on October 29, 1944. It was made by the O. C. McClintock Company of Minneapolis and was listed on the National Register.',
  'A metal clock on a base, plinth and column, topped with a copper and glass head, in the middle of Copake hamlet. The Copake Grange Hall is close by.',
  'A wartime chime clock that became the hamlet\'s emblem.',
  'In the public way in Copake hamlet; park on the street.',
  [W('Copake Memorial Clock')],
  brick='no'),
N('copake-grange-hall','Copake Grange Hall (Grange No. 935)','Copake','hud','civic','standing',42.1025,-73.55028,'site','view','drive','','1902–1903',
  'Copake Grange No. 935 built this hall in 1902–1903 and added to it in 1906 and 1921. For many years the upstairs was the public library. It was listed on the National Register in 2001.',
  'A two-storey wooden hall with a gable roof and two one-storey flat-roofed wings. Its present use and whether it opens were not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner not checked. See it from the road in Copake hamlet.',
  [W('Copake Grange Hall')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-johns-lutheran-livingston','St. John\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church','Livingston','hud','worship','standing',42.09383,-73.79642,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1901–1902',
  'A Late Gothic Revival church of red pressed brick built in 1901–1902 at 923 County Route 19, with two towers of different heights. Its cemetery holds about 2,200 burials from 1821 on. It was listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'Red brick walls on a high basement, a steep gable with a three-part pointed window, a deep bracketed cornice and two unequal towers. The churchyard beside it can be walked. Service times and the interior were not checked.',
  '',
  'Church property on County Route 19 (Elizaville postal address). Look from the road or the churchyard unless a service is on.',
  [W('St. John\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church (Livingston, New York)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('high-falls-philmont','High Falls and the Agawamuck mill dam','Philmont','hud','water','traces',42.24713,-73.65768,'area','public','hike','','early 19th century',
  'Agawamuck Creek drops 150 feet here, the tallest waterfall in Columbia County, and more than a dozen mills once stood along it; Philmont grew up as a mill village on the water. Little is left of most of them, but a dam built in the first half of the 19th century still holds the creek at the top of the falls (Uncovering New York). The land was private until the Columbia Land Conservancy opened it as the High Falls Conservation Area, and in recent years it has rebuilt the trails and added an observation deck (The River Newsroom).',
  'From the lot, the green trail leads about half a mile to the red trail and the overlook, the one official view of the falls; the old dam is seen at the top of the falls from there. About 1.5 miles of trails in all. The newer gravel route from the lot to the waterfall has an even surface, though it was not built to ADA rules (The River Newsroom). The pin is the parking lot: the falls and the dam were not located on the aerial or the LiDAR.',
  'Philmont\'s mills hung on a 150-foot fall.',
  'Columbia Land Conservancy, High Falls Conservation Area, on Roxbury Road in Philmont. Parking lot at 540 Roxbury Road (OpenStreetMap). The Conservancy\'s page refused to open in this session, so hours and rules are not checked; stay behind the overlook rails above the falls.',
  [UNY,RIVER,SH,CLC],
  brick='no'),
]
